{
   "id": "ITM-AGT-10009-12",
   "archetype_id": "BC-QA-10009",
   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A function \\( f \\) has derivatives of all orders. The first-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = -2 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(- \\frac{7}{4}\\right) \\). It is known that \\( f''(-2) = 2 \\) and that \\( |f''(x)| \\le 6 \\) for all \\( x \\) between \\( -2 \\) and \\( - \\frac{7}{4} \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         3,
         16
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 1 polynomial needs the maximum of \\( |f''(x)| \\), the derivative of order \\( 2 \\), between the centre and the input.",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - -2|^{2}}{2!} \\) with \\( M = 6 \\) and \\( |x - -2| = \\frac{1}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            3,
            16
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluating gives \\( \\frac{6 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2}}{2} = \\frac{3}{16} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            3,
            16
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{3}{16} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            3,
            16
         ]
      }
   ],
